    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `fstring_to_string`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `fstring_to_string` function converts an f-string node back into a regular string node. This is useful when you need to transform an f-string expression into a plain string literal, perhaps for analysis or transformation purposes. The function takes a single parameter, `node`, which is expected to be an instance of the `Node` class representing an f-string. It first strips the f-string prefix from the node's string representation to isolate the actual string content. Then, it creates a new `Leaf` object of type `token.STRING`, using the stripped string and the original node's prefix. The line number of the new `Leaf` is set to the line number of the original node, defaulting to 0 if the original node does not have a line number. The function returns this new `Leaf` object. The function relies on the `str()` method to convert the node to a string and the `get_lineno()` method to retrieve the line number. It does not handle any specific errors or edge cases, nor does it have any side effects.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
